    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Vice-Provost--Indigenous-Engagement_JR-1025313": {
      "title": "Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-05\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: September 29, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Senior Administrative Council (SAC) (Employee Group) Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement Brock University, Niagara Region, Ontario Brock University is seeking a respected, relationship-centered Indigenous leader to serve as its next Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement. This is a unique opportunity to help shape the next chapter of Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, and institutional transformation at Brock University. Building on a strong foundation of community partnerships, Indigenous programming, and institutional commitment, the successful candidate will play a central role in implementing Brock's Indigenous Strategic Plan and fostering meaningful, sustainable change throughout the University. The University Brock University is a comprehensive teaching and research institution with rich academic programs and world-class scholarship. Located on the traditional lands of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement, with its main campus located in the historic Niagara region, Brock offers all the benefits of a young and modern university in a safe, community-minded region with beautiful natural surroundings. With more than 18,000 students in seven diverse Faculties, 600 faculty members, and over 124,000 accomplished alumni, Brock has earned a reputation as a one of Canada’s top post-secondary institutions. The university continues be an important regional presence and with a new campus in Burlington, further growing connections to the community through this new, dynamic space. Brock was recently   named one of Canada's best employers for Company Culture by Forbes   as well as   ranked top 10 as one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2024 ,   through an independent survey of more than 40,000 Canadian employees working for companies with more than 500 employees. Brock is also home to fourteen 3M Teaching Fellowship Award Winners, Canada’s most prestigious recognition of excellence in educational leadership and teaching at the post-secondary level, and eight Canada Research Chairs. The Role Reporting to the Provost and Vice-President, Academic, the Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement serves as the University's senior Indigenous academic leader and a key member of Brock's academic leadership team. The VPIE provides strategic advice and leadership across the institution to advance Indigenous priorities and strengthen relationships with Indigenous communities, students, faculty, staff, Elders, Knowledge Holders, and partners. Key Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership and Institutional Transformation   – Provide leadership for the implementation of Brock's Indigenous Strategic Plan and related institutional priorities. Support the development of measurable goals, accountability frameworks, and institution-wide initiatives that advance reconciliation, decolonization, and Indigenous engagement across academic and administrative portfolios. Serve as an advisor to the institution, working closely with senior leaders, faculty, staff, and students to build capacity for Indigenous engagement and cultural understanding. Community Engagement and Partnerships   – Serve as Brock's primary senior contact for Indigenous community engagement. Build and maintain respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, leadership organizations, governments, educational institutions, and community partners. Support meaningful engagement with Indigenous advisory and governance bodies while helping strengthen alignment between community priorities and institutional processes. Student Success and Well-being   – Champion Indigenous student success by supporting culturally grounded programs, services, and initiatives that foster belonging, achievement, leadership development, community connection, and holistic well-being throughout the student experience. Academic Leadership and Indigenization   – Provide leadership and guidance regarding Indigenous governance structures, institutional policies, and community-informed decision-making. Collaborate with Deans, faculty leaders, researchers, and academic units to advance Indigenous curriculum development, support Indigenous research and scholarship, build relationships with Indigenous faculty and staff, strengthen community-engaged research, and promote respectful and reciprocal approaches to knowledge creation and dissemination. The Ideal Candidate As the ideal candidate, you are an accomplished Indigenous leader in the higher education sector who is recognized for your ability to build trust, strengthen relationships, and guide organizations through meaningful change. You are a courageous leader, bringing a demonstrated commitment to Indigenous advancement and possess the credibility, humility, and strategic leadership required to work effectively across Indigenous communities and complex institutional environments.   You understand that meaningful progress is rooted in both relationships and action. You are a skilled listener, collaborator, and advocate who can bring diverse groups together around a shared vision while ensuring priorities are translated into measurable outcomes. Whether your experience has been developed primarily within higher education, Indigenous organizations, public institutions, community leadership, or a combination of these environments, you bring a demonstrated record of advancing Indigenous priorities, leading organizational change, and fostering strong partnerships. Qualifications and Demonstrated Success: Indigenous identity and lived experience, preferably connected to local communities, consistent with the University's intention to fill this role as a designated appointment for an Indigenous candidate. An earned doctorate, terminal degree, or an equivalent combination of education, community leadership, executive experience, and demonstrated accomplishments. Progressively senior leadership experience within post-secondary education, Indigenous organizations, government, public sector organizations, or similarly complex environments. Demonstrated success building meaningful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, organizations, Elders, Knowledge Holders, and partners. Experience leading strategic initiatives, organizational change, and collaborative planning processes. Understanding of Indigenous education, Indigenous student success, Indigenous scholarship, and community-engaged approaches to teaching, learning, and research. Knowledge of Indigenous governance, policy environments, and contemporary issues affecting First Nations, Métis, and Inuit peoples. Experience working collaboratively within complex organizations and advising senior leaders, governing bodies, or community decision-makers. Skills and Attributes: Exceptional relationship-building and consensus-building skills and a collaborative, authentic, and community-centered leadership style. Strategic thinking and action-oriented, with strong implementation abilities. Excellent communication, facilitation, and engagement skills. Courage as a leader with a commitment to accountability, transparency, and shared responsibility. Humility, integrity, resilience, and cultural grounding. Understanding of distinctions-based approaches and the diverse realities of First Nations, Métis, Inuit, and urban, global and reconnecting Indigenous communities. Role Type: Current Vacancy The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications on September 30, 2026. KBRS does not use artificial intelligence in any part of candidate and application screening. Brock University recognizes that it is located on the traditional lands of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement. Today, Brock is the gathering place of many First Nations, Métis and Inuit — many of whom continue to live and work on its campus. Brock University is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive environment and encourages applications from all qualified individuals, including but not limited to women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, racialized persons, and persons of any gender identity and sexual orientation. Support and accommodations will be provided to applicants with disabilities, upon request. Applicants in need of an accommodation in order to participate as a candidate in the recruitment process can contact   accommodate@kbrs.ca   or identify their needs to the recruitment professional named in the job description. KBRS does not use artificial intelligence to screen candidate applications. Candidates interested in this opportunity should contact Abbey MacLeod at  amacleod@kbrs.ca , Kyle Steele at   ksteele@kbrs.ca , or Dr. Jennie Massey at  jmassey@kbrs.ca , or submit a full application package by clicking \"Apply Now\" by visiting the following link: https://www.kbrs.ca/Career/17744448320300000008kiz We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Library-Assistant--Resource-Sharing---Reference_JR-1025308": {
      "title": "Library Assistant, Resource Sharing & Reference",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: September 17, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role: Reporting to the Manager, User Services & Engagement, the Library Assistant, Resource Sharing & Reference position is a member of the resource sharing team and contributes to the daily processing of borrowing and lending resource sharing and interlibrary loan (ILL) requests. In addition, the role provides circulation, basic copying and printing services, and reference assistance to users at the Ask Us Desk and is also a member of the library’s group of Reference Assistants who provide in-depth research help to students. The Library Assistant, Resource Sharing & Reference will:  Utilize the ILS system to process borrowing and lending requests, following established procedures and manually updating/processing requests as needed;  Retrieve library stacks materials for physical loan or digitization as requested following established procedures for local lending, interlibrary loan, and course reserve;  Search library catalogue and databases for requested items, reporting access issues to Electronic Resources Management (ERM) department as needed;  Monitor shared email mailbox and respond to patron and partner inquiries regarding ILL requests, explaining procedures and policies, renewals, status and availability of materials;  Process requests for loans between campuses, to students at a distance, and to faculty and staff mailboxes, as requested;  Work with RS Coordinator to troubleshoot problems, such as problematic requests in in the ILS or dealing with lost or damaged materials;  Submit supply orders to vendors for physical resource sharing;  Reconcile credit card payments for supplies and shipping costs in Workday;  Assist users in locating, using, and borrowing library material, including books, reserve readings, audio-visual items, and other equipment available at the desk;  Provide support to users to operate equipment, such as computers, all-in-one printers/scanners/photocopiers, self-check machine, microform reader/printer; troubleshoot problems; refer to Library Systems and Technologies staff as necessary;  Maintain and update patron system records accurately, including collecting payments, creating temporary user accounts for visitors, and recording user interactions;  Provide in-depth research assistance in person and remotely by staffing the Ontario Council of University Libraries (OCUL) virtual reference chat service and the library’s primary email account as the first point of contact; delivers remote support to students, faculty, and staff across the university; and triages inquiries across the library in accordance with established procedures;  Interpret and analyze user needs and determine which sources should be consulted; refer to librarian as needed; and Instruct library users in using library collections, online catalogue, databases, Internet resources, and how to formulate search terms. Key Skills and Experience: Undergraduate degree in relevant discipline; Expertise of Interlibrary Loan systems for sharing electronic resources between libraries; Familiarity with Library of Congress, Music, and Government Documents classification systems; Technical aptitude to assist users in navigating the university website, troubleshoot common problems with equipment (print/copy/scan stations, desktop computers), use the POS to process payments, use of Brock’s financial system (Workday) and processes and frequent use of Microsoft Office suite of products for daily time management, communication, and work tasks; Strong customer service and interpersonal skills; Flexibility and sensitivity to meet the needs of a diverse user population; Ability to maintain confidentiality of patron information; Strong problem-solving skills; and Strong accuracy and attention to detail. Preferred or Asset Skills:  Library Technician Diploma; Knowledge of the responsibilities of the library departments and library staff, in addition to the services offered by Learning Commons’ partners and other University units; and Knowledge of a broad array of information resources and proficiency in searching the Library catalogue, Library website, and electronic databases to which the Library subscribes, specifically their general content and use features. Salary and Total Rewards:  Job Grade (I), Salary Scale $52,798.00 - 84,476.00; Target Hiring Range: $52,798.00 - $60,668.00*; This is a permanent position that includes:   Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage. Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan. Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays. Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program. *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Instructor-ADST-5P56-Winter-D11-ST-CATHARINES_JR-1025336-1": {
      "title": "Instructor ADST 5P56 Winter D11 ST. CATHARINES",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: September 30, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) ADST 5P56 Winter D11 ST. CATHARINES Compensation Effective September 1, 2026 - $7,887.59 per half course (195 hours nominally) Effective September 1, 2026 - $15,775.20 per full course (390 hours nominally) Please note: Instructors who are employed in a 2-hour/week lecture, or the equivalent of a 2-hour/week lecture, are responsible for the first hour of seminar/lab in each course. Duties Duties and responsibilities will be in accordance with Article 22.01 of the Collective Agreement. These include scheduled contact time with students and non-classroom time (preparation of lectures, student consultation, marking and grading and course administration, including grade appeals and cases of academic dishonesty). Qualifications 1. Completed PhD in Disability Studies, Psychology or related field. 2. Demonstrated academic teaching and leadership experience in the field of disabilities.  3. Experience in Applied Research with persons with disabilities. 4. Proven online teaching experience required – synchronous and asynchronous. 5. Available to teach primarily IN PERSON IN ST. CATHARINES* during the day on weekends (i.e. 9-5) as scheduled, some online instruction during evenings on weekdays as required, and asynchronous instruction (e.g., lectures, activities) as needed to meet course requirements. 6. Instructors are required to provide office hours for 1:1 and group student support as needed. 7. Experience using Brightspace or other learning management systems.  8. Experience using video conferencing platforms (e.g., Lifesize, Microsoft Teams) 9. Able to attend department related meetings via video conference as required. *Please Note: The mode of delivery for this course as planned for Fall/Winter 2025/26 terms are primarily in-person with a portion of additional synchronous and asynchronous online instruction during the term. It will be expected that you will be able to deliver the course material in the required format and planned schedule when the course is delivered. Applications Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide seniority date in the application questionnaire.  Assure to include all required documentation, including letters of reference and course/performance evaluations. Please Note Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval. As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement: Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time. Applicants that are in excess of the above noted workload limits will not have their applications considered unless they have received prior written approval from the appropriate Dean and the Human Resources Department. The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the Human Resources Web Site - https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/ Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Student-Accessibility-Case-Manager--part-time_JR-1025332": {
      "title": "Student Accessibility Case Manager, part-time",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: September 29, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group)   This is a part-time fixed term contract, expected to conclude in April 2027     About the Role:     Reporting to the Manager, Student Accessibility Services,  the Student Accessibility Case Manager supports the university’s commitment to equitable, inclusive, and accessible education by working directly with students who experience disabilities, chronic health conditions, mental health conditions, neurodivergence, and other barriers to participation in academic life.   The Student Accessibility Case Manager works in a team-based model to assess students’ functional needs, develop and coordinate   individualized academic accommodations, and provide ongoing advising and support. The role works collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners while   maintaining   student privacy, promoting self-advocacy, and ensuring that accommodations are implemented effectively and consistently.     As the  Student Accessibility Case Manager,  you will:      Meet individually with students to understand their disability-related experiences, functional limitations, and academic needs; Review relevant documentation and determine appropriate academic accommodations in accordance with university policies and applicable legislation; Develop, communicate, and coordinate individualized accommodation plans; Advise students on accommodation processes, academic options, campus resources, and strategies for successful participation in university life; Support students in developing self-advocacy, self-management, and communication skills; Consult with instructors, faculty and staff regarding the implementation of approved accommodations while protecting student confidentiality; Collaborate with campus partners, including academic departments, Mental Health and Wellness Services, Student Health Services, Housing, advising, and other student-support units; Coordinate accommodations such as accessible course materials, testing accommodations, assistive technology, note-taking support, interpreters, captioning, and other disability-related supports, as applicable; Respond to accommodation-related concerns and assist in resolving barriers or implementation issues; Maintain accurate, confidential, and timely student records in accordance with privacy legislation and university policies; Make referrals and/or recommendations for scholarships, awards, Ministry bursaries, and other disability-related funding supports; Participate in case consultations and contribute to complex accommodation assessments and problem-solving; Provide education and consultation to faculty, staff, and students on accessibility, disability inclusion, universal design, and accommodation practices; Provide support to parents and supporters during transition to university and disability-related needs; Contribute to accessibility initiatives, outreach, workshops, and institutional projects; and Keep current with developments in accessibility legislation, disability services, assistive technology, inclusive education, and relevant professional practices.   Key   S kills and   Experience :   Completion of a Master’s level Degree in a related industry, such as Applied Disability Studies, Social Work, Occupational Therapy or Applied Psychology or 5+ years of relevant experience in a post-secondary environment;   A minimum of 2 years of progressive and related experience, working specifically with individuals with mental health disabilities, preferably in a post-secondary environment;   Knowledge and understanding of the functional impact of a disability, related concerns and the resulting impact on student’s academic and/or class, and/or social participation in a post-secondary educational environment;   Expertise in a strengths-based approach assisting students to problem-solve learning issues, deliver solution-focused support and recommend accommodations;   Demonstrated experience delivering disability-related counselling e.g., academic transition, course specific concerns, employment issues; crisis intervention and case management services;   Ability to assess complex situations, exercise professional judgment, and make well-reasoned recommendations; Understanding of Ministry of Colleges, Universities, Research Excellence and Security (MCURES) mandates for providing Student Accessibility Services, as well as Ontario Human Rights Code (OHRC) and Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA) legislative requirements;   Effective communication skills and the ability to advocate for the needs of students with various stakeholders (including faculty, professionals, and agencies);   The ability to provide input, establish goals and participate in service direction;   Demonstrated ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information with professionalism and discretion; and Commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, and student success.   Salary   and   Total Rewards:    Job Grade   L , Salary Scale   ( $ 67,721- 108,353 ) ;   Target Hiring Range:   $ 67,721-   $ 71,000 *   prorated to reflect part-time hours   T his   is a   contract   position that   includes :      Vacation:   Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.   *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range.   We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Teaching-Assistant-LING-3P95-Winter-D3_JR-1025339": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant LING 3P95 Winter D3",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 4, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) Title of Course: LING 3P95 Discourse Analysis Compensation  Teaching Assistant, Course Coordinator and Lab Demonstrator positions are paid according to the qualifications that the employee holds.    Employees who do NOT hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the undergraduate rate - $ 23.30   per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 202 6 )   Employees who hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the graduate rate - $ 36.67   per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 202 6 )   In cases where   additional   duties are required as part of a Teaching Assistant’s duties, these hours shall be compensated at the   appropriate Teaching   Assistant rate.    Duties  The duties and responsibilities of the Teaching Assistant shall be in accordance with Article 22 of the CUPE Local 4207, Unit 1, Collective Agreement. These are made up of scheduled contact time with students and non-classroom time (including preparation of seminar material, student consultation, marking or grading, examination proctoring, attending lectures, holding office hours, and other course administration as may be required. Qualifications  Minimum four-year BA complete, or admission to 4th year with 80 percent major average Must have taken this course (or equivalent) and received 80 percent (or equivalent) in the course Demonstrated interpersonal qualities that support students’ course-related needs and that promote good communication/discussion Strong organizational skills and a demonstrated capacity to keep accurate records and to manage data, including solid practical ability with Excel Applications  Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide seniority date within the application questionnaire.   For further information  Department: Applied Linguistics Email: appliedling@brocku.ca Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval.  As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement:  Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time.   The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the   Office of People & Culture   website   -   https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/   Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
