/Lab Demonstrators/Marker-Graders and holding meetings with them; assisting in the preparation of marking schemes and coordinating the marking of course assignments and/or midterm, progress and final exams; and any other related duties as may be required by the course instructor.  Responsibilities:   In collaboration with the Course Instructor, the Course Coordinator will:   Facilitate course communication:   Respond to student inquiries via email and/or LMS   Post announcements on course LMS site   Facilitate course evaluation measures:   Format exam scripts, prepare for printing, and coordinate distribution/tracking of exam documents   Invigilate exams   Coordinate and administer make-up quizzes/exams   Coordinate grading of exams and assignments supporting consistency across multiple marker-graders/sections   Correspond with students who have missed assignments, tests, exams and assisting in the coordination of make-up assessments, as approved by the course instructor   Support academic accommodations and medical absence verification:   Liaise with Student Accessibility Services (SAS) and course instructors to coordinate academic accommodations for individual students   Validate and correspond with students regarding Medical Self Declaration and Medical Verification Forms   Prepare course materials/resources and maintain course LMS site:   Liaise with EdTech in course LMS site design and configuration   Post materials/resources to course LMS site   Support updating, formatting, editing course materials, including lesson plans, assignments, and assessments   Qualifications  The qualifications for this position have changed since it was last posted. A completed Master of Nursing (MN/MScN) degree or a recognized graduate degree in a related field is preferred  Previous course coordination experience working in a large course with multiple sections  Experience grading scholarly papers and other written course evaluations  Strong foundation in education and Learning Management Systems (LMS)  Strong organization and time management skills Applications  Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide seniority date within the application questionnaire.  For further information  Aundrea Wright Academic Coordinator, School of Nursing NUSCacademic@brocku.ca Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval.   As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement:  Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time.  The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the Office of People & Culture website -  https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/ Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Course-Coordinator-NUSC-3P01-Winter-D3_JR-1025362": {
      "title": "Course Coordinator NUSC 3P01 Winter D3",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 14, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) Title of Course: Advanced Nursing Topics 1 Course Code: NUSC 3P01 Hours: 120 hours Contract Duration: December 7, 2026 to April 30, 2027 Compensation  Teaching Assistant, Course Coordinator and Lab Demonstrator positions are paid according to the qualifications that the employee holds.   Employees who do NOT hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the undergraduate rate - $23.30 per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 2026)   Employees who hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the graduate rate - $36.67 per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 2026)   Duties  Reporting to the Department Chair or Course Instructor, the duties and responsibilities of the Course Coordinator will be in accordance with Article 22 of the Collective Agreement. These include overseeing the seminar/tutorial/lab sections for a particular course; coordinating Teaching Assistants/Lab Demonstrators/Marker-Graders and holding meetings with them; assisting in the preparation of marking schemes and coordinating the marking of course assignments and/or midterm, progress and final exams; and any other related duties as may be required by the course instructor.  Responsibilities In collaboration with the Course Instructor, the Course Coordinator will:  Facilitate course communication:  Respond to student inquiries via email and/or LMS  Post announcements on course LMS site  Facilitate course evaluation measures:  Support readiness of in-person seminar and simulation sessions, including materials, room and lab set-up, and attendance tracking for graded participation  Coordinate documentation of missed seminars and simulations and any approved accommodations, consistent with the course's mandatory-attendance and drop policies  Coordinate grading of the written assignments and the weekly seminar and simulation participation grades, supporting consistency across marker-graders and TA-led sections through shared rubrics and answer keys  Correspond with students who have missed assignments, seminars, or simulations, and assisting in the coordination of make-up assessments, as approved by the course instructor  Coordinate the simulation and seminar components: Confirm students complete required pre-simulation virtual work and support compliance with Simulation Lab. Distribute the TA facilitation manuals, simulation facilitator guides, and answer keys, and brief TAs and facilitators to ensure a consistent, standardized student experience across the nine seminars and three simulations Support academic accommodations and medical absence verification:  Liaise with Student Accessibility Services (SAS) and course instructors to coordinate academic accommodations for individual students  Validate and correspond with students regarding Medical Self Declaration and Medical Verification Forms  Prepare course materials/resources and maintain course LMS site:  Liaise with EdTech in course LMS site design and configuration  Post materials/resources to course LMS site  Support updating, formatting, editing course materials, including lesson plans, assignments, assessments, the TA facilitation manuals, simulation facilitator guides, weekly activity materials, and answer keys  Qualifications  A completed Master of Nursing (MN/MScN) degree or a recognized graduate degree in a related field is preferred  Previous course coordination experience working in a large course with multiple sections  Experience grading scholarly papers and other written course evaluations  Registration in good standing with the College of Nurses of Ontario (or eligibility) is an asset, given the clinical and simulation content of the course Familiarity with nursing simulation and lab-based learning, and with coordinating standardized, TA-led seminar delivery across multiple sections Applications  Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide seniority date within the application questionnaire.  For further information  Aundrea Wright Academic Coordinator, Nursing NUSCacademic@brocku.ca Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval.   As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement:  Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time.  The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the Office of People & Culture website -  https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/ Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
