    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Gender-and-Sexual-Violence-Research-Assistant--_JR-1025404": {
      "title": "Gender and Sexual Violence Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 15, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Part-Time Contract Positions (Employee Group) Gender and Sexual Violence Research Assistant     Student Employment Opportunity: Human Rights and Equity     Anticipated start date:  October 12th, 2026    Positions available:  1   Hourly rate:  $17.95/hour (5-10 hours/week)    Joining the Human Rights and Equity Office     The Gender and Sexual Violence (GSV) team is a part of Brock’s Human Rights and Equity (HRE) Office. HRE supports students, staff, and faculty by addressing human rights, harassment, and discrimination concerns that are connected to the Ontario Human Rights Code and by promoting equity, inclusion and belonging across campus.     We are seeking one Research Assistant to support the Manager of Gender and Sexual Violence Intake in the review of Brock University’s Sexual Violence Policy. The Research Assistant will assist with coordinating focus groups with staff, faculty, and students, including dedicated affinity spaces to support participants in sharing perspectives within communities of shared identity or experience. The role will also involve qualitative data analysis and contributing to the development of a final report summarizing the findings and recommendations.   The successful candidate will be offered a 60-hour contract from October 2026 through December 2026 . They will be expected to be available for  in-person program delivery  at the St. Catharines campus of Brock University.   The successful candidate will report to and work with the Manager of Gender and Sexual Violence Intake.   For more information about the HRE office, applicants are encouraged to review the  HRE website .     Duties & Responsibilities   Research & Data Collection   Support the Manager of Gender and Sexual Violence Intake in the review of Brock University’s Sexual Violence Policy.    Assist with the planning and coordination of focus groups with students, staff, and faculty to gather feedback and perspectives on the Sexual Violence Policy.    Attend focus groups as a research assistant and take detailed, accurate, and objective notes to document participant feedback, discussion themes, and key insights.    Support affinity-based focus groups by providing dedicated note-taking and research support in spaces designed to allow participants with shared identities or experiences to contribute their perspectives.    Assist with the development and organization of research materials, including recruitment materials, discussion guides, participant information, and note-taking templates.    Support participant recruitment and outreach in collaboration with campus partners, student groups, and underrepresented communities.    Data Analysis & Reporting   Organize and review qualitative data collected through focus groups and other feedback mechanisms.    Assist with identifying key themes, patterns, and areas of concern emerging from participant feedback, with attention to diverse and intersectional experiences of gender-based and sexual violence.    Support the development of a comprehensive report summarizing research findings, key themes, and recommendations to inform the review and revision of Brock’s Sexual Violence Policy.    Maintain organized and confidential research records in accordance with applicable privacy, ethical, and institutional requirements.    GSV Research & Learning   Review relevant research, policies, and best practices to support the analysis and interpretation of findings.    Contribute to discussions with the Manager of Gender and Sexual Violence Intake regarding emerging themes, research findings, and implications for policy and practice.    Outreach & Engagement   Liaise and collaborate with campus and community partners, student groups, and underrepresented communities to support research participation and engagement.   Develop promotional and recruitment materials to support awareness of the policy review and opportunities for students, staff, and faculty to participate.    Support presentations or other engagement activities related to the policy review and research findings, as appropriate.     Qualifications     Experience     Understanding of gender-based and sexual violence and a commitment to supporting survivors    Commitment to ending sexual violence as demonstrated through previous or current professional, community-based, or volunteer work is strongly desired    Previous work/volunteer experience in the GSV field is appreciated but not required. Area of academic concentration is not a factor in this hiring.     Preference will be given to individuals with lived experience as members of Indigenous, racialized, 2S&LGBTQIA+, and disabled communities, those who have experienced GSV, those with lived experience of sex work, or other marginalized communities.     Eligibility     Must be a current Brock University student as of September 2026    Provide a clear Vulnerable Person Police Check upon hiring    Required Skills     Strong interpersonal communication skills    Documentation and reporting skills    Ability to follow protocols for reporting incidents and uphold confidentiality    Developing educational messaging    Outreach and engagement skills    Critical thinking skills    Time management    Ability to work in collaboration with a dynamic team and take initiative to lead projects      Please note: this role may involve exposure to sensitive information related to gender-based and sexual violence. Support is available as needed.       Eligible candidates will receive an email to arrange a virtual or in-person interview.         We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Budget-Officer_JR-1025420": {
      "title": "Budget Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 23, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role:    The Budget officer provides expert financial support for the Faculty of Education, ensuring effective budget development, monitoring, and compliance with institutional policies. The Budget Officer combines analytical and operational responsibilities, including forecasting, variance analysis, transaction oversight, and teaching load reconciliation. They act as the faculty’s primary financial resource, supporting academic leadership with data-driven insights and ensuring efficient financial operations.    The Budget Officer will:     Prepare financial forecasts and conduct variance analysis on actual-to-budget comparisons;  Conduct financial analyses, projections and reporting on course enrolment, including low enrolment analysis;  Investigate significant variances and provide actionable recommendations to improve performance;  Maintain accurate and timely reporting for faculty leadership, including trimester projections and consolidated financial summaries;  Perform financial analysis for faculty projects, and program accounts;  Review and approve faculty financial transactions, ensuring adherence to university policies and funding requirements;  Process journal entries, reconcile accounts and monitor deficit/ended accounts  Provide guidance to faculty and staff on financial policies, expense categories, and best practices;  Coordinate and prepare the faculty’s annual budget submission, working closely with academic units and administrative staff;  Develop supporting schedules and templates for budget processes, ensuring accuracy and compliance with university guidelines;  Provide financial input for Faculty planning initiatives and advise on budget impacts of operational decisions;  Support special projects and initiatives, while providing finance and accounting expertise; and  Identify opportunities for process improvement and contribute to operational efficiency.    Key Skills and Experience:    An undergraduate degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or related discipline or a combination of education and experience;  Strong knowledge of financial systems, reporting tools, and Microsoft Excel;   Skilled in budgeting, forecasting, and variance analysis;    Promotes and embodies inclusivity and respect within the workplace and the broader community;   Strong communication and interpersonal skills;    Works effectively with faculty leadership, staff, and central services;     Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and organizational skills;     Ability to interpret and apply policies and provide guidance to stakeholders;    Ensures accuracy in financial reporting and compliance;     Provides practical solutions to financial and operational challenges; and    Responds effectively to changing priorities and deadlines.      Salary and Total Rewards:    Job Grade [K], Salary Scale ($61,286.00 - $99,384.00  Target Hiring Range: $61,286.00 - $65,000.00  This is a 12 Month contract position that includes:    Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage.  Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan.  Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.  Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program.  *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Graduate-Physics-Preparation-Instructor-PHYS-5N02-Winter-D3_JR-1025429": {
      "title": "Graduate Physics Preparation Instructor PHYS 5N02 Winter D3",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 15, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) Compensation Effective September 1, 2026 - $7,887.59 per half course (195 hours nominally) Effective September 1, 2026 - $15,775.20 per full course (390 hours nominally) Please note: Instructors who are employed in a 2-hour/week lecture, or the equivalent of a 2-hour/week lecture, are responsible for the first hour of seminar/lab in each course. Duties The nominal workload for this position is approximately 195 hours per semester.   Reporting to the Physics Graduate Program Director, the Graduate Physics Preparation Instructor will develop and deliver a preparatory course for new graduate students in Materials Physics (MSMP) whose first language is not English. The objective of this course is to enable development of English language skills in a science, especially physics, context, prior to proceeding to the MSMP courses. The course should cover the following topics: (i) critical thinking and synthesizing arguments and ideas, (ii) researching, referencing and academic integrity, (iii) academic presentations and discussions, (iv) development of specialized vocabulary, and (v)  academic note taking.   The instructor is responsible for the preparation of weekly lesson plans, tests, and assignments, in accordance to the requirements of the MSMP curriculum. The instructor is also responsible for the marking and evaluation of student work. Additionally, the job will involve coordinating the orientation sessions for the MSMP students with ITS, Library, and other relevant Brock services and facilities, including laboratory safety training.  Duties and responsibilities will be in accordance with Article 22.01 of the Collective Agreement. These include scheduled contact time with students and non-classroom time (preparation of lectures, student consultation, marking and grading and course administration, including grade appeals and cases of academic dishonesty).  Qualifications In most circumstances, the minimum qualifications shall be: (1) Bachelor degree in a relevant field; (2) a background in science and scientific writing shall be taken into consideration; (3) post-secondary teaching experience, especially in  ESL/EFL, is very desirable; (4) demonstrated interpersonal qualities  that support international students’ course-related needs.  The applicant must demonstrate successful experience in teaching at a university level.    Applications Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide their seniority date in the application questionnaire.   For further information     Please contact:   Ivana Komljenovic Metcalf  Physics   ikmetcalf@brocku.ca     Please Note     If applicable, applicants are responsible for submitting their seniority date with their application. Applicants that don't include a seniority date will be assumed not to have one.    Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval. As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement: Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time. Applicants that are in excess of the above noted workload limits will not have their applications considered unless they have received prior written approval from the appropriate Dean and the Human Resources Department. The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the Human Resources Web Site - https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/ Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
