    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Associate-Director--Experiential-Education_JR-1025418": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Experiential Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 18, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role:  Reporting to the Director, Co-op, Career, & Experiential Education, the Associate Director, Experiential Education works in partnership to translate CCEE's strategic direction into effective Experiential Education (EE) operations, initiatives and partnerships, while bringing forward evidence, emerging opportunities and recommendations to inform portfolio-level planning and decision-making. The incumbent leads a complex, multidisciplinary team working across diverse Faculty and academic contexts. The incumbent provides direction that supports consistency and alignment across the EE portfolio while recognizing the distinct needs, relationships, priorities, and operating contexts of individual Faculties. The incumbent oversees experiential education policy, programming, operations and continuous improvement, and represents the University on matters pertaining to experiential education.     The Associate Director, Experiential Education provides leadership and direction to the EE team, establishing shared priorities, decision-making approaches and ways of working that support effective collaboration, accountability and service across the University. The incumbent is also responsible for creating and nurturing relationships with the broader team of Co-op, Career and Experiential Education (CCEE) and other critical internal and external partners to the University, including community organizations and other academic institutions. They promote knowledge mobilization and management of day-to-day operations alongside supporting CCEE’s leadership in co-operative, career and experiential learning.    The Associate Director, Experiential Education will:   Develop and uphold strategy to engage and retain local, national and international organizations for the purpose of developing curricular-based experiential learning partnerships;  Provide leadership and guidance to EE staff in navigating complex Faculty, departmental and institutional relationships;  Ensure that Faculty relationships and individual portfolio approaches remain connected to broader CCEE and University priorities;  Work with community and institutional partners to identify appropriate approaches to evaluating partnerships and EE initiatives, and use findings to strengthen relationships, programs and outcomes;  Oversee and ensure collaboration with cross-functional multi-disciplinary experiential education staff and Faculty to maintain consistent practice, data reporting and tracking;    Establish shared approaches, principles and decision-making frameworks that promote consistency across EE while allowing appropriate flexibility;  Lead the identification, development, implementation and evaluation of initiatives that improve program quality, partnerships, student and partner satisfaction, staff capability and performance;    Ensure equity, diversity and inclusion best practices are embedded at the foundational level of all experiential education services;  Oversee and ensure the maintenance and engagement with a database of EE opportunities at local, national, and international levels;  Develop and oversee appropriate approaches to evaluate EE programs, services, partnerships and initiatives;  Analyze and interpret quantitative and qualitative evidence to assess program effectiveness, identify trends and opportunities for improvement, and inform strategic decision-making;  Manage the Experiential Education budget and contribute to the CCEE annual budget planning process, aligning resources with established strategic priorities;  Translate CCEE and University strategic priorities into operational plans, initiatives and priorities for Experiential Education;  Oversee the administration and stewardship of external funding received to support experiential education initiatives;  Ensure alignment of EE work, resources and processes across Faculties and departments while recognizing appropriate differences in Faculty context and program needs.    Key Skills and Experience:  Experience in the field of experiential education, adult education with program and policy development;  Experience in a leadership and/or supervisory role, including strategic planning functions;  Demonstrated experience leading multidisciplinary teams in complex organizational environments with multiple partners and competing priorities;  Strong understanding of experiential education theories and the strategic role career and experiential education has in a university environment;  Demonstrated effectiveness in adult education, teaching and facilitation;    Strong interpersonal and organizational skills, with the ability to communicate with executive level internal and external partners;  Demonstrated knowledge and experience in program evaluation, assessment and continuous improvement, with the ability to use evidence and feedback to inform program and service development;  Competency with budgeting, data, strategic, and financial planning;  Promotes and embodies inclusivity and respect within the workplace and the broader community;  Demonstrated ability to build and maintain collaborative relationships with faculty, partners, and local, provincial, national, and international partners while serving as a liaison between the University and external communities;  Strong analytical, critical thinking, and problem-solving skills;  Proven ability to manage multiple projects, deadlines, and priorities independently while adapting to change, ambiguity, and evolving organizational needs;  Ability to leverage resources effectively, conduct research, disseminate knowledge, and support continuous learning and improvement;  Strong organizational, time management, prioritization, teamwork, and integrity-focused work practices.  Salary and Total Rewards: Job Grade O, Salary Scale ($90,294- 144,470); Target Hiring Range: $90,294-$104,000* This is a permanent position that includes:  Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage.  Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan.  Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.  Flexible Work Arrangements: Hybrid work schedule available.  Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program.  *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track--Early-Childhood-Education-and-Care--Equitable--Inclusive--and-Sustainable-Practices_JR-1025406": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Early Childhood Education and Care: Equitable, Inclusive, and Sustainable Practices",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nThis position is part of the BUFA (Employee Group) Brock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: Note to all candidates: This posting will close at 12:01 am on the date listed . November 13, 2026 About Brock University The Brock University experience is second to none in Canada. Located in the historic Niagara region, Brock offers all the benefits of a young and modern university in a safe, community-minded city, with beautiful natural surroundings. With over 18,000 students and more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in seven diverse Faculties, Brock excels at providing exceptional experiential learning opportunities and highly rated student and campus life experiences. Our Geography Brock University’s main campus is situated atop the Niagara Escarpment, within a UNESCO World Biosphere Reserve, overlooking the city of St. Catharines, in the heart of Niagara wine country. The Niagara region is dotted with landmarks that recognize our nation’s history and features breathtaking natural beauty and world-famous attractions. St. Catharines is home to vibrant arts and entertainment venues, and is a short drive from Toronto, Niagara Falls, and Buffalo, New York. With one of the warmest climates in Canada, clean, safe communities, and surprisingly affordable real estate, Niagara is an exceptional location to call home. What we Offer Brock University offers competitive salary and benefits and ample support for research and sabbaticals. Research resources include; conference support, start-up funding, subscriptions to major databases and access to various research funding vehicles. For candidates considering relocation, moving expenses will be administered according to the Faculty Association Collective Agreement. About the Faculty The Faculty of Education at Brock University, with a proud history dating back to 1965, is home to nearly 33,000 alumni and currently serves a vibrant community of undergraduate and graduate students across its St. Catharines and Burlington campuses. Renowned for its excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, the faculty offers a range of innovative programs. Our mission is to educate exceptional educators by fostering academic excellence, promoting experiential learning, and engaging meaningfully with Indigenous and community partners. Our faculty members are committed to scholarship, teaching, and service that foster anti-racist, decolonial, and sustainable communities—communities that affirm human and nonhuman onto-epistemological diversity and advance equity. In alignment with Brock University’s strategic plan (2025-2030), this commitment is reflected in efforts to promote inclusion, reconciliation, accessibility, and decolonization across teaching, research, and community engagement ( https://brocku.ca/strategic-plan/ ). About the Bachelor of Early Childhood Education Program The Bachelor of Early Childhood Education (B.ECE) Honours program is a college-transfer undergraduate degree designed to support the continued growth and professionalization of early learning and care professionals committed to reflective, ethical, and evidence-informed practice. This online asynchronous program prepares educators to critically examine and reconceptualize conventional understandings of early learning and development, equipping them to engage thoughtfully with diverse perspectives, challenge inequitable practices, and contribute to more just and sustainable early childhood education and care (ECEC) communities. The program is unique and heralded both within the university and nationally as a quality program that positions students with the skills and competencies needed to meet the escalating labour market demands within the Canada-Wide Early Learning and Child Care (CWELCC) agreement system. About the Position The Department of Educational Studies invites applicants for a Tenure Track Appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning January 1st, 2027. Area of Expertise Within an ECEC context, equitable, inclusive, and sustainable practices encompass pedagogical approaches, policies, and early learning environments that promote equity, inclusion, social responsibility, cultural integrity, and environmental stewardship for children, families, educators, and communities. These practices uphold children's rights; value diverse identities, perspectives, and ways of knowing; address systemic inequities; and foster belonging, participation, and collective well-being. Examples of relevant areas of study may include: equity, social justice, and inclusion; environmental and sociocultural sustainability; broader structural, political, and community impact; ethics, futures, and intergenerational responsibility; anti-biased education and respect; agency, participation, and citizenship. Responsibilities of this position include: Teaching in the B.ECE online asynchronous program including, but not limited to, one or more of the following areas: Indigenous Culture & ECE; Ethics, Policy & Care in ECE; ECE for Sustainability. Commitment to supporting the quality of the B.ECE program and Department of Educational Studies, including commitment to Indigenous practices and decolonial education; equity and inclusion; justice, ethics, and respect. Graduate and undergraduate student mentorship and supervision. Establishing and maintaining an innovative and productive research program in ECEC. Service contributions to the department, the university, and ECEC community. Qualifications The Department of Educational Studies seeks candidates who are committed to equitable, inclusive, and sustainable practices in ECEC through research, teaching, and service. Required: PhD (or ABD) or equivalent in Early Childhood Education or closely related field (e.g., Early Childhood Studies, Curriculum and Pedagogy in the Early Years, Educational Studies with a focus on Early Childhood) or equivalent Indigenous knowledges1, specializing in Early Childhood Education. Demonstrated commitment to the field of ECEC, advancing equitable, inclusive, and sustainable approaches, including, but not limited to, anti-oppression, decolonial, reconciliation, anti-racist, anti-homophobic, anti-transphobic, anti-xenophobic, gender equity, and socioeconomic justice through course design, research endeavours, and teaching approaches. Successful teaching experience in ECEC at the post-secondary level. Professional experience in ECEC settings, programs, or services (e.g., child care, pre-school, pre- kindergarten or kindergarten2, community programs, extended care programs for school-aged children, child and family services). Familiarity with the ECEC field's ongoing challenges and emerging directions in relation to research, theory, practice, and/or policy. Preferred: Proficiency in inclusive, accessible, and effective course design and asynchronous digital pedagogy, with awareness of the implications of artificial intelligence for teaching and learning. Established connections with the local, national, and/or international ECEC community at the scholarly, governmental, and/or practice levels. An ECE Diploma, recognized professional ECE Certification or registration, or a comparable ECE- specific credential from another jurisdiction. 1. In determining a Ph.D. equivalency for Indigenous knowledge, consideration shall be given, but not limited to, the level of knowledge of the language and/or traditional customs including protocols, spirituality, traditions, practices, ceremonies, histories, and teachings of a particular group of Indigenous people or peoples. This knowledge may have been acquired through lived experience; active and lengthy participation in Indigenous forms of self-determination and governance, cultural structures, and processes; and a careful study and reflection of their philosophical underpinnings. In many cases, acquiring this knowledge will have involved studying with an Indigenous Elder or Traditional Knowledge Carrier/Keeper.   2. For experience obtained within kindergarten settings, the applicant must have practiced in an early childhood educator role.   What to Include in the Application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter (~3 pages) demonstrating the candidate’s alignment with the posting. Teaching Dossier that includes: a teaching statement (~2 pages) that articulates the candidate's philosophy, experience, and pedagogical approaches to preparing ethical ECEC professionals. The statement should demonstrate commitment to relational and culturally sustaining pedagogy, diverse Indigenous and non-dominant knowledge systems, and transformative practices that promote Indigenous knowledges, sovereignty and relationships and Equity, Diversity, Inclusion, and Belonging; and evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., sample course outlines and assessments, mentoring experiences, student and/or peer evaluations). Note. We recognize that teaching evaluations are not necessarily indicative of one’s teaching strengths and capacity. Therefore, candidates need not submit evaluations but ought to demonstrate teaching impact and effectiveness in academic and/or non-traditional educational contexts. Research Statement (~2 pages) describing the candidate’s program of research, major contributions, and future research plans. Three Referees : Names and contact information for three academic referees (who will only be contacted for short-listed candidates). All documents must be submitted in electronic format (a single well-organized PDF document is preferred) via the online application system (file maximum of 5MB per attachment upload). The closing date for applications is November 13, 2026 at 12:01am. The final full day to apply is November 12, 2026. Inquiries should be directed to Wanda Burger, Executive Assistant, foedean@brocku.ca. The target hiring range for this position is $ 100,000 to $118,000. The final salary will be determined based on relevant experience, internal equity and budget considerations. This ad is for an existing position in the Brock University Faculty Association (BUFA) bargaining unit. This position is subject to final budgetary approval. Our Commitment Brock University is actively committed to diversity and the principles of employment equity and invites applications from all qualified candidates. Women, Indigenous (First Nations, Métis, Inuit) peoples, Black people, people with disabilities, members of visible minorities/racialized groups, and Two-Spirit, lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer, intersex, and additional sexually and gender diverse (2SLGBTQI+) persons are encouraged to apply and to voluntarily self-identify as a member of a designated group as part of their application. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups should fill out the Self-Identification Form and include the completed form with their application. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. We will accommodate the needs of the applicants and the Ontario Human Rights Code and the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA) throughout all stages of the selection process, as outlined in the Employment Accommodation Policy https://brocku.ca/policies/wp-content/uploads/sites/94/Employment-Accommodation-Policy.pdf . Please advise: talent@brocku.ca   to ensure your accessibility needs are accommodated through this process. Information received relating to accommodation measures will be addressed confidentially. We appreciate all applications received; however, only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca"
    },
